From 6202c843a9b065e81dfe84508c2c691b1f6b1689 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher AMIAUD <christopher.amiaud@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 13:19:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] Stop the other threads of a forked process when it exits

With threaded fork(), the threads of a child process are host threads
of blink, so they outlived it: exit() left them running or waiting,
along with the copy of the address space they use, and a fatal signal
asked them to stop, which they didn't notice while waiting, since no
host signal interrupts their wait. The parent then waited forever to
join the thread which started the process, if that one still waited:
a shell hung when a thread of btop crashed, or when a thread called
exit() while the main one read a pipe.

exit_group() now calls KillOtherThreads() for child processes too, and
CheckInterrupt() and the loops that wait for i/o, children or SIGCONT
end their wait once their thread is killed.

The table of processes also no longer takes a process for the thread
which started it: when that thread is gone, another one stands for it,
and the cpu time of a process counts all its threads, past and present.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
